le.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${60}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}}=\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba003d7-6822-5351-9fe7-349e06fc8124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579044} {:answer/id #uuid "4c491272-cad8-5a95-9ff9-8d544bca7e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579103} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc326e4-39aa-59d0-9995-1c21de4103c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579215} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d46ad2-0466-5bcb-b853-ea0fc19ba251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579250} {:answer/id #uuid "f21cb5bf-3232-5255-a94f-7f607f76cb25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f21cb5bf-3232-5255-a94f-7f607f76cb25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579378} :answer-field/id #uuid "949a1db0-2e58-54bd-8e7a-b416759e3f8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4e2b7c0-aa7f-5e60-b7cc-99adc6546180" :question/math-academy-id "q-64924" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-\\sqrt{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c87004-23ba-569a-9c9b-b89eeccfb56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579024} {:answer/id #uuid "42121e6e-9031-571f-8fc2-a00e89928f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579089} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f40621-37d9-5f8d-9408-bcc23b9c495d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579277} {:answer/id #uuid "f6569e0e-bfee-5cd3-8a9a-d1d7a6c6953a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579387} {:answer/id #uuid "f8741f6f-93ed-597b-88dd-a0aa8ad5a364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8741f6f-93ed-597b-88dd-a0aa8ad5a364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579388} :answer-field/id #uuid "85afbf53-a50a-529d-b696-6bb998e797a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee81d71b-117d-5b90-9487-69a61ec1a15b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64942" :question/problem "Determine the value of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-{210}^{∘}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{210}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n-{210}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={150}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-{210}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cot}⁡({150}^{∘}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${150}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{150}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡({150}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘})}=\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a06c524c-97c9-571d-887e-2ec12c21b024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579244} {:answer/id #uuid "bea5dc77-ebd1-5a6b-b874-2871bcab41e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579295} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b0e014-e415-57b4-bd55-9cb7c771d508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579328} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe42df2-c1cd-5100-a74d-8965f74b92d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579334} {:answer/id #uuid "ef30e1c5-71ec-5346-81a5-60e9cafefea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef30e1c5-71ec-5346-81a5-60e9cafefea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579369} :answer-field/id #uuid "54f20341-5524-513a-9b1a-88215ae1d662" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdd7cf3f-8536-5ff0-aafa-89d7ca6fc326" :question/math-academy-id "q-37931" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{\\pi }^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+y)]}_{\\pi }^{7\\pi /6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi +y))\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi +y))\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+y)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi +y)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi +0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{17\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002ae2a2-57f7-5488-af75-79fb59c74df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}-12\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579677} {:answer/id #uuid "15516f3a-c0ed-5bbd-a792-fe9f912b1913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579714} {:answer/id #uuid "a65dbf10-14e5-5728-a726-d6b72f27f37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579960} {:answer/id #uuid "db52c63a-c0e1-5e36-b3a2-1810317849d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580051} {:answer/id #uuid "db6401c8-086a-509a-a29a-23ad1c9142f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}+2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db52c63a-c0e1-5e36-b3a2-1810317849d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580051} :answer-field/id #uuid "eef8c0f8-5bf3-5ab4-88d2-624213e64652" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20e2fb06-75fc-5406-b1dc-e81b5249a3bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-103201" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3},$ then $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-12\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-6$ into the left-hand-side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x &=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-12\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3},$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-12\\pi )=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-12\\pi )=6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3})=-2\\sqrt{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3227c650-6c66-5a55-9504-c55173bdeabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579763} {:answer/id #uuid "399e497e-7edf-5c8b-bb71-12243da891a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579776} {:answer/id #uuid "410d14d8-629c-5258-95ff-63054dd04390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579793} {:answer/id #uuid "640244e9-5910-5ff4-93e8-90b28ad3fa1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579841} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6daca6-1a59-52f3-af4c-eca50384843a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "399e497e-7edf-5c8b-bb71-12243da891a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579776} :answer-field/id #uuid "a400fe05-b0fe-5f9a-a033-2cc157672ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31d526c9-cd4d-5afd-b547-b21b29aa4a23" :question/math-academy-id "q-73558" :question/problem "${\\int }_{3}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}({5}^{y-3}\\sqrt{x})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{4}({5}^{y-3}\\sqrt{x})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{5}^{y-3}[{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{5}^{y-3}{[\\frac{2}{3}{(\\sqrt{x})}^{3}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{5}^{y-3}(\\frac{2}{3}{(\\sqrt{4})}^{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{(\\sqrt{0})}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{16\\cdot {5}^{y-3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{16\\cdot {5}^{y-3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{16}{3}{[\\frac{{5}^{y-3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}(\\frac{{5}^{4-3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}-\\frac{{5}^{3-3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}(\\frac{5-1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d033f63-1d66-5ce2-bcb1-637c021ea285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579696} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4328c5-5492-5329-9034-e97e26c1a982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579754} {:answer/id #uuid "464e29c0-4ca0-5be3-aa58-9dd2759740f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579801} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc6b00c-4eaf-56a8-867d-290428433023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579814} {:answer/id #uuid "d159542f-d8cb-5fd5-a9b0-7f44e7e3e2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fc6b00c-4eaf-56a8-867d-290428433023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579814} :answer-field/id #uuid "8abad105-d627-54a6-90a0-f416d5cd11ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53b06cb2-e2f8-59e9-806d-b2e5cf57453e" :question/math-academy-id "q-113749" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{3}+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2{x}^{3}+4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2{x}^{3}+4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 6 & 20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+4}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{6}^{20}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{20}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{6}^{20}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{6}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{20}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0edd0587-b80c-5a5d-b074-82d25d6a73de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579700} {:answer/id #uuid "757341cc-68c9-5023-95d1-db7937d02e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579869} {:answer/id #uuid "78fd0479-6fc9-5117-a735-fefbc3024eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579880} {:answer/id #uuid "97af7509-7658-573b-b435-5060c5a50991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579931} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4618cb-d781-50ed-992b-dfa48edfd64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "757341cc-68c9-5023-95d1-db7937d02e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579869} :answer-field/id #uuid "21f7bbfd-ac9d-5ccd-abdb-e44df258f098" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64a73ec8-a2f2-57ef-9a04-50abdd49fead" :question/math-academy-id "q-103208" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ then $-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi )+1=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-7$ into the right-hand-side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x &=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi )=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi )+1 &=-10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})+1=2\\sqrt{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2121c2f9-54ec-5628-b7cc-ae06f7d0ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579736} {:answer/id #uuid "534d4abb-6da7-5ef7-8ba7-4e7aeb98d90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579820} {:answer/id #uuid "6dff903f-e185-584b-8b74-2e2a864cccbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579858} {:answer/id #uuid "78d516f5-dbe2-52c8-aa16-48cd5d4e15fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579879} {:answer/id #uuid "c89d9057-063c-56b1-99b9-dee392a92ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2121c2f9-54ec-5628-b7cc-ae06f7d0ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579736} :answer-field/id #uuid "e741ec4d-b33f-59f0-952c-d99444a295ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ea78e8a-2d26-5cb2-90ce-10f5dc605094" :question/math-academy-id "q-136542" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡a=-\\frac{1}{3},$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/08/08d442eff9febe44bd39e6c15c998d76d56d17bbb7a676d2d511771479bb2f29.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡a \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101503d8-ac1e-519c-b643-3145ef500675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579702} {:answer/id #uuid "34532bb4-3b6e-51fa-b8f4-8f1df6ae53b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579768} {:answer/id #uuid "6d52c566-ed29-5bd3-b9c4-4764a5933e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579857} {:answer/id #uuid "d064234d-5324-5917-96ae-62821d5c1cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580026} {:answer/id #uuid "e7cfb4b2-5304-54e2-9284-c03e92de6812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d52c566-ed29-5bd3-b9c4-4764a5933e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579857} :answer-field/id #uuid "f95b7983-f575-5576-8124-46c508261fb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90429821-063a-5b87-91d0-cc785ad38d6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126175" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0, \\\\ {x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(\\frac{1}{2}<X\\le 2).$" :question/worked-solution "We can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}<X\\le 2) & =P(X\\le 2)-P(X\\le \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =F(2)-F(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ca860ca-1bce-5c95-bf20-df60d5073a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579942} {:answer/id #uuid "ba27bc13-aa91-5810-af6d-8927013670e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579986} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6f8c5c-a483-504f-bde0-740e5465f479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580021} {:answer/id #uuid "dccc9183-fffa-5efb-b96a-bba2f1ebfef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580056} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c9a237-8761-5b58-a458-0ac3e2d56369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba27bc13-aa91-5810-af6d-8927013670e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579986} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbbf5b5a-e886-5680-94b8-ec7a52bf455e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a4ada4a1-8379-5844-873d-7530ca6e23ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-102371" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x)}{g(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & ={\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x)}{g(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{g(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x))}^{′}}{(g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{′}(x))}^{′}}{({g}^{′}(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{g}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1-4}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x)}{g(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}=\\frac{3}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7c3bba-a4de-5381-8288-8b3a3666d70f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/725098acf4bef4d9d34f45e8dcf3d44bca342cb4490380a5de6993778bcec0cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579731} {:answer/id #uuid "3213cd65-1687-527e-b318-aa93d61e04f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/174432d35b30691b54762c41be2e5587e64eee45c36a235622cf7d3c6d69357e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579762} {:answer/id #uuid "64a26e6d-e6ea-566b-bb6e-208885d72cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4f469e6a205f530e9b98ee3ef061cb7f938e86d214f1a3ed41d5b963ef9bab4f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579843} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5bc494-b70c-566f-8e3e-3820e7c02d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/70d802eaac422bed7450db74e99cae4598fe27c498b6d75f5ff858513cc5a4e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579972} {:answer/id #uuid "d05a986c-cf21-501f-a198-58ddfa0888d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4b/4b64bf094402621345fbca574c29d9ffe2dbc6de7d6c95c90d37898aab3b006a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d05a986c-cf21-501f-a198-58ddfa0888d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4b/4b64bf094402621345fbca574c29d9ffe2dbc6de7d6c95c90d37898aab3b006a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580025} :answer-field/id #uuid "712048fa-195a-5653-b798-8a02ed257182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcd6b9ef-34af-5d07-a2d1-6c14934637fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-70292" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}),$ which of the following shows the curve $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "In order to graph the function, it's helpful to know the period. In order to determine the period, we first need to determine the scaling factor.\n\nNotice that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})$ can be written as $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}x).$ So, it takes the form $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)$ with $B=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nThe horizontal scaling factor is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{B}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{5})}=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\n\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}=\\pi \\cdot 5=5\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow that we know the period is $5\\pi ,$ we can draw the general shape of the tangent function, but stretch it so that it fills up the interval $(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ec/ec77ab8cf1ed3d66c0715abbe4f803d41cb876363307bc3f4e27394c0161053d.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39421a8e-415f-5a66-b400-40a2817f0153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39421a8e-415f-5a66-b400-40a2817f0153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579775} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ff9c1d-3810-5604-a0fe-70128423e48c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c74e516b-0f8d-5d67-a6fa-3383a90be5db" :question/math-academy-id "q-262452" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡0+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡0+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡0=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡0+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0+0=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e38b72a-de59-5cd2-876c-41cdfae845ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9a/9af8a23313cbcfea757befb3a135869fb724b44af4d7d3e3ee9d5cd5db821453.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579698} {:answer/id #uuid "1a612360-627a-593c-bdb0-763542c7b05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bcccc090807ef73b87b4fe2f8950ab057bf484c85a27cec55c04108f58fb4438.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579722} {:answer/id #uuid "327fdac1-d5e9-5ac7-9c96-644217dc4cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f4eff259ad6aaf200a274b1fb0972b4850837ffa1fd40ebf517f0c4f77956ec5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579764} {:answer/id #uuid "3521a9e2-c8f7-518c-83f0-67697f00841f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/270e32d36fbc20004ff91af1f73fad320af720f81b9e7c092f335e22a4f7e8b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579769} {:answer/id #uuid "601ef556-5aab-56d8-a151-b02e7ed977a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/21e32cb520b6057503db999ec156399680480a27f04aa50290b56818483766cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a612360-627a-593c-bdb0-763542c7b05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bcccc090807ef73b87b4fe2f8950ab057bf484c85a27cec55c04108f58fb4438.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579722} :answer-field/id #uuid "0115aeb9-8988-53ae-aa65-c5fb88987f84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc96eed1-562d-564e-989f-42cdc2296582" :question/math-academy-id "q-72759" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}),$ which of the following shows the curve $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "In order to graph the function, it's helpful to know the period. In order to determine the period, we first need to determine the scaling factor.\n\nNotice that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})$ can be written as $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x).$ So, it takes the form $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)$ with $B=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe horizontal scaling factor is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{B}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\n\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}=\\pi \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we know the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we can draw the general shape of the tangent function, but stretch it so that it fills up the interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/189aa1ea4e33d47bd26027af4990020c837211a58d53668e25f9210353faa784.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c93837-d2f5-58bc-a841-935f7b08b658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579685} {:answer/id #uuid "1e680472-4d5f-554e-a921-972ebea0b7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579730} {:answer/id #uuid "4103d277-f890-5702-906a-f19c16ce9abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579792} {:answer/id #uuid "71b5c202-b054-5832-a969-d7b23f696a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579865} {:answer/id #uuid "7e73025a-f67c-522e-baa4-aa14f1358ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4103d277-f890-5702-906a-f19c16ce9abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579792} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7d6eb5a-a310-5061-85c8-964416a21fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9752437-70b8-5a90-968d-cf807ea578b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-72047" :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(0-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fb603f-99e1-55bc-9588-77e136b79039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580450} {:answer/id #uuid "492a6a5d-3a6f-5f71-b86d-8254bca584d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5566" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580510} {:answer/id #uuid "5403b4f9-e3bd-50a9-94a2-ee5fe8970f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0906" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580527} {:answer/id #uuid "6b537724-55d3-50f0-841c-0c2a4b421cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6650" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580568} {:answer/id #uuid "9661636a-57b9-51c0-bed2-cb7d7f17f0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0656" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9661636a-57b9-51c0-bed2-cb7d7f17f0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0656" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580640} :answer-field/id #uuid "4029d8d3-196b-5f66-b128-4c9c3eb421c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "052845df-31c1-5ac6-8fdb-db6eae2b480b" :question/math-academy-id "q-108283" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ calculate $P(X<3).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x≈6.30168\n$$\n\n*The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{96}\\cdot 6.30168 \\\\  & ≈0.0656\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114cd42d-ba52-5869-9448-93a17d0920de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580410} {:answer/id #uuid "4edb41e5-8763-5f56-92a0-923aac665da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580521} {:answer/id #uuid "921a2907-c9c2-55f3-a5c1-c088eea2d222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580634} {:answer/id #uuid "d55a31f3-9f9c-5cf2-9b86-12761d10e402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580719} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7d360d-043f-5158-a0ad-2486d0c89e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4edb41e5-8763-5f56-92a0-923aac665da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580521} :answer-field/id #uuid "1acf62d4-21d8-564e-9008-9e94eb8fcbe4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d4c2c34-0e64-5420-b51f-0cd0b469fb94" :question/math-academy-id "q-13524" :question/problem "Calculate the integral ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3b08c5-67ec-556a-9db8-346afb23990d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d3b08c5-67ec-556a-9db8-346afb23990d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580406} :answer-field/id #uuid "04e9c3ac-e070-5fba-b495-8a7967767527" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ccd5c9d-8efc-5890-b6e0-7b6adea1884d" :question/math-academy-id "q-350055" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /12$ | $\\pi /9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /4$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u){|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =2(2-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =4-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f3138bb-1417-57f0-b00e-e24da7b370ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f3138bb-1417-57f0-b00e-e24da7b370ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580434} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f0429a-8ccc-52fa-a654-3ae343603cc2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2018f257-8b60-511d-90d3-af91acbd86bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-350039" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=5x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c31c8f6-d9d8-53c5-b2d5-4515c416102e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580428} {:answer/id #uuid "5de5a8c0-40fe-5080-8dcd-16fa4bea9378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580544} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9a5de0-f659-5f3b-acd0-b7b08a36484f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580628} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c414a5-ad71-5780-8d90-e3844295abee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580715} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6e8ea5-4e2c-5195-ac1d-c3213d882366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5de5a8c0-40fe-5080-8dcd-16fa4bea9378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580544} :answer-field/id #uuid "75e64c80-fb76-53ed-a478-ee475bda1a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26a6ef81-4a35-59c2-9081-35e0aed550d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-49545" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/16)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/16}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(x/4)}^{2}}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580897 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8233fce-5fa1-504d-a858-b5359a37944d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8233fce-5fa1-504d-a858-b5359a37944d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580778} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2d8f522-565f-5640-81d7-21d42b1024a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ed18e1b-5d9e-5d88-b764-bf87259e7a34" :question/math-academy-id "q-350908" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x)}{2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x)}{2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ee8018b-4c2c-5969-bab1-98bc54c84ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580546} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6ca949-563a-5a3e-845e-2d7ec457bf56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580566} {:answer/id #uuid "7494aed9-29fc-5421-9cd6-437ef544113d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580590} {:answer/id #uuid "82b2c57d-6347-5d32-9f6a-25b6b00af7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580610} {:answer/id #uuid "de4fd513-ada9-5107-a3f8-6bff6ac94e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a6ca949-563a-5a3e-845e-2d7ec457bf56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580566} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdf91fbd-c400-5c7a-a3c0-a17b20ba8a84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48ce0cc1-da3f-5716-9932-2b23b9354edd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117619" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca9f4065965fc7d50c0c19087172fbb7591d573bdc571bd1477737eaef8161ff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡Bx+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. Given that $B>0,$ what is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\pi -(-3\\pi )=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the period, we get\n\n$$\n4\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{2\\pi }{4\\pi }=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nComparing the graphs of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ and the above, we see that the above graph is a vertical translation of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ by $3$ units up. \n\n![](images/2f/2f898960995c28b1a084dd6a19afdbc275259c411350de62bcaf6061df3f2240.png)\n\nSo, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+3.\n$$\n\nFinally, $B+D=\\frac{1}{2}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9862e91c-0efe-5bb8-8925-45bc31839846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9862e91c-0efe-5bb8-8925-45bc31839846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580645} :answer-field/id #uuid "65d0530e-816a-5976-ad52-1c7704a78646" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49d4a023-e567-5ef0-bff2-0a05874f4d54" :question/math-academy-id "q-350892" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157ba3a6-4cca-5941-a534-c9c20fa09bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580420} {:answer/id #uuid "3949c7e0-bdb2-5737-8510-f0d11b3691e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580482} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef527f8-4905-5cfc-a055-ae7479e16a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580490} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d38d57-8f2d-5651-9702-d6a449719bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580664} {:answer/id #uuid "c919e599-9d99-561f-836d-c7fe18e1e60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ef527f8-4905-5cfc-a055-ae7479e16a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580490} :answer-field/id #uuid "54d8cdce-d6e8-500b-b341-549a9fb54bae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4cef5015-939e-5574-8406-e8591217fe5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-117641" :question/problem "![](images/68/68cc2e6c02238663e248316b63a462c32a5225842728c563adc738cdcb42e417.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/56/56b1b17826611d0a0b63f24342094d0ef99e9c6c8428829b4a2bdd3593e0caa7.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $4$ to give $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally,  stretch it parallel to the $x$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ to give $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23bed5a7-946a-5761-b3c6-c169f961d99c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{4}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580446} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3f4fae-e3f0-5ab1-aeab-69c1e6b34bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580458} {:answer/id #uuid "4d845e5f-48d1-56a8-a9a9-92289951f110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580518} {:answer/id #uuid "7895e320-c61b-57ba-84d3-b9a150bd1eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580593} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7089bb-a038-5d2b-a34b-9bf3c599cdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d845e5f-48d1-56a8-a9a9-92289951f110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580518} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd737fdb-a1db-508c-9c67-7a25aa8d3a5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51526ec7-ea58-5f68-9175-85663bcbb887" :question/math-academy-id "q-108284" :question/problem "Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ what is $P(X<3)$ expressed as an integral?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f7f8af-dd67-585c-8b4c-22006bfdb024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02f7f8af-dd67-585c-8b4c-22006bfdb024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580389} :answer-field/id #uuid "3805d3f0-30ff-5610-aef3-e77ea0b75940" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6196cc63-e579-5b66-890d-b1daabdef7d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-350036" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{2\\pi x}{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45daf244-4c3f-5472-93a8-da7d6c3bdccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580505} {:answer/id #uuid "52196bc9-8d38-5c9d-83d2-2c96f288c9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580525} {:answer/id #uuid "b5431721-a52a-5556-b365-52a555669b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580682} {:answer/id #uuid "d171db58-409b-5e77-8fc1-6b70a1ac11c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580716} {:answer/id #uuid "e3853a5f-a3bd-5d18-8620-4554383b00ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5431721-a52a-5556-b365-52a555669b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580682} :answer-field/id #uuid "1444f051-3ccb-51c7-99bf-4f860a1031ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86968101-fa3c-5286-a648-9b995fa5fe70" :question/math-academy-id "q-114003" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+1)=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u={cos}^{3}⁡x+1.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $9/8$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{9/8}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{9/8}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{2}^{9/8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{8})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{8})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{(\\frac{9}{8})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d020a63-ff8a-5103-950e-32a99925bad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d020a63-ff8a-5103-950e-32a99925bad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580596} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a470524-f944-5b81-ad61-34953fcf821f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891837cc-fc66-582c-9fc5-1ebbcc953137" :question/math-academy-id "q-350053" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /9}3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /18$ | $\\pi /9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /9}3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}3{sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23957abc-dcc1-5b7b-b7fd-001f7457d167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580445} {:answer/id #uuid "982579e3-83e0-5545-ad26-b221c0744545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4x}{5})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580644} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd6becb-70ef-5fc9-86e0-64d33c238ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580709} {:answer/id #uuid "d79deab6-1af2-5703-a987-c1a179e4bf66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580725} {:answer/id #uuid "f00b9b0f-679b-51f4-a9d8-05b26e14d8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f00b9b0f-679b-51f4-a9d8-05b26e14d8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580767} :answer-field/id #uuid "192e842c-aa36-501f-8fbd-126807e8ffc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9198c09e-55b1-58ef-a675-b557b8606f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117650" :question/problem "![](images/67/67e90ea05c5461f3e569fcced387784d8a2e3c3779976825324137d94d85bad6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/20/2090e695566d64097143c31d83e17ea965cd899a2ed3e91536242f84bf538961.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $x$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{5}{2}$ to give $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}).$\n\n- Finally, shift it **up** by $3$ units to give $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+3.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eba5ea3-c760-543a-9428-7f559f0c5c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580462} {:answer/id #uuid "35bf6bf5-de1e-5a2d-bdb4-baab3ce1b96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580475} {:answer/id #uuid "7eeaf67a-e2d5-5432-a061-9f5572d2c070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580601} {:answer/id #uuid "9c486bb7-eca1-5a11-b1ef-4e49680dd755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580651} {:answer/id #uuid "d034f1a9-1ab7-5477-9331-e04bc9fd60fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c486bb7-eca1-5a11-b1ef-4e49680dd755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580651} :answer-field/id #uuid "54a50cd2-7f18-53e5-8d18-76399b8b3490" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "941badbf-4e01-5ab0-9ce7-1e744c3a6f57" :question/math-academy-id "q-117590" :question/problem "![](images/53/5356d2cd00eb22d232c03e41be0a03ffc71d3410b3e5d2db6a4e925b9f0f29b1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+D.$ What is the value of $D?$" :question/worked-solution "The constant $D$ is the vertical shift of the function.\n\n![](images/23/230b2c0476083f8c8faf35e4a762f30e4c43af5fefbdff67b691e58da5a63dd0.png)\n\nTo plot the given graph, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Stretch it horizontally by a stretch factor of $2$ to give $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}).$\n\n- Then, shift it by $2$ units up to give $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+2.$\n\nTherefore $D=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "128f2af3-d86a-521d-9b87-d08c1d096374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580412} {:answer/id #uuid "280defab-bb0e-5467-9302-2304ccfe9c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}-2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580453} {:answer/id #uuid "e145844b-7979-5eb0-a240-56b9f9f42d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580741} {:answer/id #uuid "e94091f4-18d7-5f6c-9b7f-e55c81c8c49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580754} {:answer/id #uuid "ef19b835-2f51-52cc-a2f9-fc1904cea930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e94091f4-18d7-5f6c-9b7f-e55c81c8c49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580754} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c9d1da-6b89-5ab2-8c06-0bef819742ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95cc69d7-bf4f-55ce-b10c-871c735fbc08" :question/math-academy-id "q-65178" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ then $\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2\\pi )-2=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-1$ into the right-hand-side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2\\pi )-2 & =\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}{3}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb570ab-f713-58af-8821-db9de526244a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580430} {:answer/id #uuid "89e2788b-34dc-5c10-832e-c1e26cda4948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580623} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bf6e52-fa08-58e9-a395-24f75244ae4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580699} {:answer/id #uuid "dc991d06-75b6-59a0-8f86-83aa43c8ae91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580733} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c5999e-67f2-524d-837b-e1e9119ae608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cb570ab-f713-58af-8821-db9de526244a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580430} :answer-field/id #uuid "518a0764-b651-5e30-a0c2-82729d5799f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a5d9405-e6fe-5a73-8a90-ca57e1597a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-117118" :question/problem "![](images/40/40322f60d8d97a7c8beff78627e78bf730be621675300b897b636a1e7d4979cf.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's add the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ to the plot.\n\n![](images/07/07415c592730ede309f0ba0979d203337e4cfcf2ebfa3b7215a27cedf7f6673f.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $\\frac{3}{2}$ units **left.**\n\nTherefore, the given graph is $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{3}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a0e0ac-e727-5461-b976-19c124b8b464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580388} {:answer/id #uuid "4890fcde-2797-5117-8088-b582d7722617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580508} {:answer/id #uuid "6d616f73-2fa0-5847-8b9f-9a01caad223b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580574} {:answer/id #uuid "cb597cd5-c5ef-508d-a0c1-618aa29883dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580705} {:answer/id #uuid "e36ac8bb-58e1-59ed-a6aa-fe6e8c50369c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb597cd5-c5ef-508d-a0c1-618aa29883dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580705} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6e41e02-7a09-5398-bc9b-a3b506f57d51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9cfe1a63-f205-5796-abd7-66f03dc0449c" :question/math-academy-id "q-13655" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3041c79b-6e78-5fa3-b00e-6bdf88d26a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580467} {:answer/id #uuid "39ef5c6b-d0ab-5cba-92ac-955caaab795b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580485} {:answer/id #uuid "91c2deee-4294-5cbc-abc1-cc4197e05f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580633} {:answer/id #uuid "9afd962e-7297-5ee9-bb39-09ac0280e0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580648} {:answer/id #uuid "fd44ae82-1421-5b26-baf9-b60ca1cd7fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd44ae82-1421-5b26-baf9-b60ca1cd7fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580787} :answer-field/id #uuid "d376e0d4-0d69-5658-b561-f7eed6420bfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f249aac-7cbc-5db4-94cc-8029f3c1555f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117133" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5be745626347b7056d054a221f05353b0da19b09093389be55b70ff6d1131083.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's add the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ to the plot.\n\n![](images/d4/d454fce5ce753dada69e9c927279237760cb32434cb4743bad04c62f10798cc9.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $\\frac{3}{4}$ unit **right.**\n\nTherefore, the given graph is $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{3}{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "151e86b6-8350-5aa4-a3a6-fbb354525ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580416} {:answer/id #uuid "153031d5-06ee-5864-aaf1-7e3797acbcaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580418} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0207e2-b065-568a-bf91-9f6dd9a3a7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580539} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5c99af-debf-5fad-8201-b22a60857246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580649} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1ef8f4-a34d-5fe2-9ed4-6536a59930aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "151e86b6-8350-5aa4-a3a6-fbb354525ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580416} :answer-field/id #uuid "b84f9b78-c8a9-5865-af16-52c04753c6de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b10e9e5c-0af8-57a2-8504-99b776b739fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-65643" :question/problem "![](images/10/10676bab3ebb60268879642c837f68e55997d4c649d010addb6e4d64ad7ed1bb.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4}),$ where $A$ is a positive constant. What is the value of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The constant $A$ is the amplitude of the function. \n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is ${y}_{max}=10$ and the minimum value is ${y}_{min}=-10.$ Therefore, the amplitude is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{y}_{max}-{y}_{min}}{2}=\\frac{10-(-10)}{2}=\\frac{20}{2}=10.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067f6ec7-2c26-556a-b275-e470f79fe0d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580398} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8770f4-ae4b-5603-ac25-45beafa1ecd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580409} {:answer/id #uuid "2991ad70-06b1-5108-bde4-df2572be3338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580454} {:answer/id #uuid "43221ccd-9516-5e86-b6d5-e44a6d2ae848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580499} {:answer/id #uuid "b42bc523-d2c6-590a-bad6-1c58f9a14c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e8770f4-ae4b-5603-ac25-45beafa1ecd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580409} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcd09329-146d-5461-81c4-c8ae03d7acd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b618bbba-0151-5ccc-837c-c0d410af93f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76235" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\nI. $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$\n\nII. $g:Z\\to Z$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n\nIII. $h:Q\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)=-2x$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not*  a surjection.  There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In fact, ${2}^{x}>0$ for all $\\in R.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Z.$ There is no number $x\\in Z$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt[3]{2}\\notin Z.$\n\n- III is a surjection. Given $y\\in Q,$ choose $x=-\\frac{y}{2}\\in Q.$ Then $h(x)=h(-\\frac{y}{2})=-2\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{2})=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5a38d2-5ac3-59e4-b944-d3d0d9cc05e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580457} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb882bb-be71-5c59-a634-65b02781c9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580522} {:answer/id #uuid "60405895-e035-58cb-842d-10b09a8a4932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580549} {:answer/id #uuid "bf692bc8-d65d-5ceb-9734-cda57421cb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580691} {:answer/id #uuid "dab99a72-47a9-51d7-b3b5-d9c4ea5ed620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fb882bb-be71-5c59-a634-65b02781c9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580522} :answer-field/id #uuid "016466b0-e060-5aab-b591-d71625582e46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c579c1f3-be8e-5aac-8600-10eab58ffbc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-49549" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{16\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/16)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/16}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e040bd-d2d8-5b84-97d3-e62216ecff52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580452} {:answer/id #uuid "36b7e2f4-59b9-5bdf-9c4a-7c0ca5ef3c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580478} {:answer/id #uuid "92f3b206-d8a5-5493-bb6a-dd1eaa00549e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580636} {:answer/id #uuid "a6065baa-6d98-5e0c-9f9f-ea438af26e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580665} {:answer/id #uuid "ee423b76-2fdd-5613-b424-50b7839fb2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36b7e2f4-59b9-5bdf-9c4a-7c0ca5ef3c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580478} :answer-field/id #uuid "629a59e6-4934-5c6a-88e2-14351298e9b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7ee7f5e-2027-5516-b089-c04ab6a54516" :question/math-academy-id "q-6506" :question/problem "![](images/53/5369c10e92ebfcb584885b4f5622dcbb817b6d7d51869cefe0fddcf76d372530.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's add the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ to the plot.\n\n![](images/da/da921ea4084bc5568d744a1394e6021de70ed9884aadd0fa1a3d9a2f65f5303b.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $4.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a07989-d7b7-5dff-b703-3bfdca7714a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580385} {:answer/id #uuid "045c6482-91c7-5d6e-9772-0f006eaf5d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580392} {:answer/id #uuid "0a5faef1-427d-52c7-a142-952b50f145d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580404} {:answer/id #uuid "4c53b5b1-1213-5925-8366-669db02e6039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580515} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff7e09d-c627-5462-9c56-8b370684b47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff7e09d-c627-5462-9c56-8b370684b47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580602} :answer-field/id #uuid "79c2e4c9-1b1b-52f5-a6fb-ffa253311b4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0a45f95-dc7e-5123-8ee5-1bed639f2d79" :question/math-academy-id "q-117037" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of $f(x)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcf5bd62bbcaee664ceb36404d3d31d42e726e52c250141a12a9c841563ce039.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is ${y}_{max}=\\frac{5}{2}$ and the minimum value is ${y}_{min}=-\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the amplitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Amplitude } & =\\frac{{y}_{max}-{y}_{min}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{2}-(-\\frac{5}{2}))}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54980c57-8d6e-54ce-afcf-f92faebaf38b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0389" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580528} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3743e5-6d0b-53aa-987b-d95480c8639f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.9393" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580565} {:answer/id #uuid "af01cbd5-6518-5f2a-a3d6-f10e4118cdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3689" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580676} {:answer/id #uuid "bac8021b-3e1c-5ea3-bfa4-7891ea89dbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0889" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580687} {:answer/id #uuid "f35dde90-b67c-53f7-bf21-15cec35bcef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9389" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f35dde90-b67c-53f7-bf21-15cec35bcef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9389" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580773} :answer-field/id #uuid "46d3ab4e-f9cb-5529-ad5e-f05fc5c34187" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f47d7654-b908-545b-ade8-3e63f03d90b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-108278" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ calculate $P(X<9).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{9}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x≈3.75560\n$$\n\n*The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(4).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4Γ(2)}x{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(2)=1!=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<9)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9) & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{9}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 3.75560 \\\\  & =0.9389\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f53716b-6ffc-5a43-86b7-02a120f702be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "972\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581277} {:answer/id #uuid "b8bdbc0c-6b3a-512e-a90d-95b8d572d5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "486\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581363} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e751fb-b23d-5f2a-800e-9805878a7260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{2}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581364} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6fd5ec-70ae-52b8-a638-6400fd60083b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581403} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a1d720-ced6-5baa-b7db-d9001c88dca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{114}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e751fb-b23d-5f2a-800e-9805878a7260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{2}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581364} :answer-field/id #uuid "309d9b32-b404-517e-8f46-72c1e1571381" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "060f3b8f-d17d-5300-84f8-5aeff0a0cf31" :question/math-academy-id "q-13254" :question/problem "What is the volume of a sphere whose diameter is $9\\,\\text{m}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{9}{2})}^{3}=\\frac{243}{2}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fbf3bdb-d682-542a-b7d1-0d0e52f2c9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fbf3bdb-d682-542a-b7d1-0d0e52f2c9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581278} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaea6bc1-cb8d-531e-8457-12e6ac63b967" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "186af47f-3a57-5366-9f75-e8f0c479aca8" :question/math-academy-id "q-315672" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical tank with a base and a lid. The tank must have an exact volume of $6\\pi \\,{ft}^{3}.$\nTo make the tank, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a metal sheet that costs $\\$4$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a metal sheet that costs $\\$1.50$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the base and lid.\n\nWhat is the radius $r$ of the tank that gives the smallest possible manufacturing cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/55/55de2c06ea57724513e450cbf49a0477d63cd75bac601d5d95f0ba7e366852ff.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =(2\\pi rh)\\cdot 4+(2\\pi {r}^{2})\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =8\\pi rh+3\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $6\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n6\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{6}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =8\\pi r(\\frac{6}{{r}^{2}})+3\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{48\\pi }{r}+3\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{48\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+6\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{48\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+6\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 6\\pi r & =\\frac{48\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 6\\pi {r}^{3} & =48\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ r & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<2$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>2$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=2$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the tank with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=2\\,\\text{ft}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a971ed0-a36e-5345-b09b-da5132f2f95a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581028} {:answer/id #uuid "597fe98d-30bb-5605-85f8-ce97c1ed4171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581185} {:answer/id #uuid "d03d220f-34c5-5ba8-aa85-e2c606284352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581415} {:answer/id #uuid "d5dc8a4c-4f29-56e2-8cd6-aadce37378dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581425} {:answer/id #uuid "f9768244-4c0d-5c1d-ad2e-719f518751a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d03d220f-34c5-5ba8-aa85-e2c606284352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581415} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ce18b2-b32f-56e5-b39c-657a343d2b69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "250351fc-aec4-5c02-8966-039220e88aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-65539" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bff2545f0d8b624d647b656502473692b2ad1e19df9733e01b14bc944355d2b2.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)-3,$ where $B$ is a constant. What is the value of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "We can calculate the period $T$ by computing the difference in the $x$-values between two consecutive asymptotes. Therefore,\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x)-3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db68153-71e4-53f2-b5a4-e679e239b458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{196}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581063} {:answer/id #uuid "33ca4d42-f211-5f5a-a904-08fe1bb8b9ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581109} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2398cc-13db-5b4d-aa48-fa2b5af29aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581128} {:answer/id #uuid "56663b96-4b26-5926-8e50-3dc0cefa35e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581177} {:answer/id #uuid "90ddd770-dc0f-545f-b30c-4fd6c7938c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{68}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a2398cc-13db-5b4d-aa48-fa2b5af29aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581128} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea381cac-6ebc-5b25-9bac-19b3cbcfbdd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26ca37f6-adee-50b5-bfff-61ba9660bf70" :question/math-academy-id "q-90561" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}x\\cdot (\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8})|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{32}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12})|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}-{(\\frac{14}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2909c7e4-d3e2-5867-adc8-3d74664805a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581086} {:answer/id #uuid "33d0c8cd-7323-53c7-9446-573596db2ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581110} {:answer/id #uuid "4006675c-b8b1-5b93-87c6-a159306806c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581136} {:answer/id #uuid "d5be0175-5c15-5047-a8f8-9ea5f240b27e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uui