dent :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581580} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c7c79f2-f158-5f61-9a43-582d495a59f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581130} {:answer/id #uuid "86c36f2a-528d-5e6d-8ed0-d9f80b110de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c7c79f2-f158-5f61-9a43-582d495a59f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581130} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3b634c9-2ce2-5afa-b86c-064a513a2509" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581608} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33da9741-b0ba-5cef-aa9a-2a26bca9dfec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581111} {:answer/id #uuid "4af3324d-1bef-5772-b8b3-526a023ab803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581155} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8487af-1d51-5564-bac5-db1559230aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33da9741-b0ba-5cef-aa9a-2a26bca9dfec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581111} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef98c78f-30a1-5090-bead-df06f712233c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581630} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51b4631c-ca96-5b6e-a5cf-8fa729ed92bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581166} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c74e77-ebf9-51a3-905a-c45c8015fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise smooth" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581357} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc4ec1c-4ff9-5e3c-bdf1-69e4df5832fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4c74e77-ebf9-51a3-905a-c45c8015fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise smooth" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581357} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6fe1777-0bd7-5bda-8b95-18c04d61f8ec" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c1a2140-4e79-5356-94be-d94c0df51bbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-343240" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-5{x}^{2}+2$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-10x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-5{x}^{2}+2∼-13+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-10x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-13+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-5{x}^{2}+2.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-5{x}^{2}+2) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-13)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -10x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -10x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-60(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ -10x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{60(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "352cd4fa-d886-55d2-bdb1-89058f24c395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581116} {:answer/id #uuid "6528a101-6796-5c43-857f-9f895a29d761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581202} {:answer/id #uuid "ae65e855-30f3-5b9e-ba89-1a9c683cb08d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581342} {:answer/id #uuid "c87a2573-4317-5af2-91e8-b7f7ba782b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581395} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a902da-c2a6-5e49-a44d-112724a28544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae65e855-30f3-5b9e-ba89-1a9c683cb08d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581342} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f17e15-9e56-5eee-aa0d-e6dbce1b4f99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a053b5b5-cfb1-5eaa-ade2-573c6fe57bdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-90566" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{6},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{27}, & 0<x<3, \\\\ \\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}, & 3\\le x, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{81}|}_{0}^{3}+{(-\\frac{9}{x})|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{3}-{(\\frac{11}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{83}{36} \\\\  & ≈2.31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c8110a-1adf-5bc3-983f-0347f7993390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581073} {:answer/id #uuid "26e0cb5d-7d5b-5a97-9d63-5d792d09ef77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581081} {:answer/id #uuid "4425904b-417a-543e-98f3-5df989edccfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581143} {:answer/id #uuid "9aaa5dcf-7a6d-5404-9621-db3dab588692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581303} {:answer/id #uuid "a54ade67-b118-5fc4-a316-6a5acb75b930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4425904b-417a-543e-98f3-5df989edccfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581143} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af379a8-42ed-5381-990e-16a10ea32720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a198ff49-4229-5ae2-be1a-5061f942656a" :question/math-academy-id "q-118440" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5a893624b5dd480d0be467c4e0947447e5527c4477e115402e5b08f89da17f8.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could be used to describe the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d5/d52b7e3ce47e0c710fc120337af1fd6a6435ad2a555289877e8752bd6bfbf873.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it across the $y$-axis.\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x).$\n\nNotice that, because the sine function is odd, the $y$-axis reflection has the same effect as the $x$-axis reflection, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)=-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ However, $y=-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is not among the given options."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8cd71ab-47df-5569-b283-26c01213e10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8cd71ab-47df-5569-b283-26c01213e10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581397} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5c37e9-7096-5259-984d-dfda4c9baeee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a49b6457-0f05-59d9-855e-88b85b73a0a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-167586" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We take the constant factor out of the integral and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{(-2+1)}}{-2+1})+C \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1})+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {x}^{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4539edfb-7a1d-5bf2-b2c7-ddb08260b99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581144} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0d25d7-0d73-551b-93a6-2c3a7b50d28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581189} {:answer/id #uuid "707edfdd-251b-50f6-a957-c041260d019f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581217} {:answer/id #uuid "c49568f3-1567-5606-af8d-6751c9f9d77f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581385} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cbc6f6-6f2f-53f6-9395-bfee7e97edd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c0d25d7-0d73-551b-93a6-2c3a7b50d28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581189} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6e8be44-96b8-594c-8db4-2d385e669e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb1876bd-8c7e-5167-ada2-d0847ddb9893" :question/math-academy-id "q-118456" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f311adf716d9fd0c0c798ec07988837ace5fcfde99ca3bdc2bba277b641b642c.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph above." :question/worked-solution "![](images/2e/2efc55d4cb4b69db92f1e80760677d432ea947f79871ae481fbbabd63efd2aac.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $\\frac{5}{2}$ units **up** to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{5}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0b2fd43-76b1-520b-8885-257c74a76cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581316} {:answer/id #uuid "c69ce661-936b-50e6-a844-3548ae845666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581391} {:answer/id #uuid "d03068c2-b1e8-5a6e-9e33-1c7d33a7f96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581414} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5b0540-6522-5291-88f0-a7cf4728b698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581471} {:answer/id #uuid "fa742624-8db8-591b-b4bc-1799172e7b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d03068c2-b1e8-5a6e-9e33-1c7d33a7f96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581414} :answer-field/id #uuid "2480749c-7cb2-5d28-bd67-b89d3b9aa890" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5e8ba75-88fa-5048-ab75-1e503c4e8a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-30975" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=-2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration,we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{-2}{2}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}){|}_{0}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007eaeb8-14c0-54d8-9ce1-9056ea0b0899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581011} {:answer/id #uuid "06721671-5f82-5c44-8cfe-6956d65c4637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06721671-5f82-5c44-8cfe-6956d65c4637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581022} :answer-field/id #uuid "2737d128-5fed-5e90-848e-82b65bc88e0b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581526} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e325977-99a8-5e05-881e-683d00a60c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581097} {:answer/id #uuid "81c9ffd0-8a6c-59ba-ba8a-f7ea201fb040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581248} {:answer/id #uuid "8666f2d5-9903-530a-b542-cb135059ad07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise smooth" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8666f2d5-9903-530a-b542-cb135059ad07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise smooth" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581256} :answer-field/id #uuid "a04825ad-fcb8-557e-ab3c-71ccf6d748b5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581582} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22a55dd7-6a06-59f8-8d5d-82dbf641a28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581072} {:answer/id #uuid "458e2880-ae71-5872-aca9-2040a5418aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581145} {:answer/id #uuid "505049f6-5344-5eda-b76d-34fdc5cdf430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581165} {:answer/id #uuid "b39f6bfa-02c8-566f-8aaf-e04c4cebd768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n+1}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "458e2880-ae71-5872-aca9-2040a5418aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581145} :answer-field/id #uuid "d358c84d-90aa-5cca-880f-3513565740e8" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4163fb57-31c2-5330-b52a-7c97d63dd851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{nx}{\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581139} {:answer/id #uuid "998e9c84-07f1-5866-8857-c772a161e2df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581297} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea27a85-b223-58af-9ce2-2c8ffc0c3c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{nx}{\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581311} {:answer/id #uuid "efd439b7-0e0c-5e26-a92f-4622b357068d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efd439b7-0e0c-5e26-a92f-4622b357068d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581473} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea4c0ec3-d5a0-5da3-93d4-34edf6f69698" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581623} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417618ba-4d18-593b-af12-2845e5661cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581140} {:answer/id #uuid "a168eb97-5941-50ea-bdef-d32c7d62f8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581320} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ef5faf-8b05-5fa9-8db8-b30a874eb1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a168eb97-5941-50ea-bdef-d32c7d62f8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581320} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ef5656-5be4-5024-85cd-c0bf053adf94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6b1f5e0-69da-58db-8d3f-4361c4c25f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343249" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+1$ defined on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-9x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+1∼1-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-9x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\pi$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-\\pi ,\\pi ),$\n\n- continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-\\pi )=F(\\pi ),$ if we extend the value at $x=\\pi$ using the formula $F(x)=-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+1) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{18(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)) \\\\ -9x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (-n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)) \\\\ -9x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-18(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ -9x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df06baf-1fbb-5246-92b0-cb7b75690161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581914} {:answer/id #uuid "5526d38f-b003-598d-8237-0a1bacc2eca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581972} {:answer/id #uuid "6301e06b-79b8-5afe-bd88-e89a20cebe81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581989} {:answer/id #uuid "bff72a75-557d-55a3-a23c-104a62c3e6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582140} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3f0e1b-8771-5f66-916e-7ee5324688d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5526d38f-b003-598d-8237-0a1bacc2eca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581972} :answer-field/id #uuid "5676ed12-b133-5752-8488-d20d47601c64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32dc9035-9034-5945-a0bd-1763fe468983" :question/math-academy-id "q-80786" :question/problem "Consider the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(xy+yz+1)}^{3}=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,1,1),$ what is the value of $\\frac{a+b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1).$ Then, since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(xy+yz+1)}^{3}=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1)$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).$    \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot y)i+(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot (x+z))j+(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot y)k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }i+\\frac{x+z}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }j+\\frac{y}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1+1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.$ \n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+b}{c}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09feefae-2675-511a-8d9c-cbfa7d941d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581871} {:answer/id #uuid "33aaccad-902f-5c71-88ea-5cd6f9d0c24d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581923} {:answer/id #uuid "41d69caa-86a1-5498-953d-3f022425261d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581940} {:answer/id #uuid "5ecdc613-cd47-5cc2-b3ec-3d75e8f827a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581984} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1af3cd-033e-5e42-9078-171f5054d688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33aaccad-902f-5c71-88ea-5cd6f9d0c24d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581923} :answer-field/id #uuid "2056bfd4-ece9-56c5-85af-50eb7d6b321d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56acb745-5864-54c2-97ff-c273d78a7989" :question/math-academy-id "q-67725" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /4)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /4)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbed3c96-9c53-52fa-88ed-a825c45fe766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbed3c96-9c53-52fa-88ed-a825c45fe766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582157} :answer-field/id #uuid "85780264-aaa0-5112-ade9-378d8daf069c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5eed33cc-e3c0-5c88-ad05-d61a465f6016" :question/math-academy-id "q-339260" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /6)^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /6)^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0debc6d4-5b95-574d-8fe1-e56c2ef3bb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581877} {:answer/id #uuid "12171151-d893-5200-8d8c-e4f4245ac463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581885} {:answer/id #uuid "7a98644d-765d-5095-93be-174c1f323301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582028} {:answer/id #uuid "a117a2b0-1b36-5d04-8126-578ff3e6c4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582090} {:answer/id #uuid "ba114fb3-8907-53ae-8480-b6bc5af5bb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a117a2b0-1b36-5d04-8126-578ff3e6c4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582090} :answer-field/id #uuid "d62efb92-c8e0-5ab5-a4f5-4d5c07b40991" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83609aa0-4b6e-5103-abe9-fb75cf3c0340" :question/math-academy-id "q-47082" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{-5x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{-5x} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}]}^{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{32})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{32})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/16/169fc7a2326d1b8bec63fd4e29bbe2dfb8f8f075682be053ec2c7f6311564fe9.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{32})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{-5x}=\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f88e25f-bbe5-55c8-a67a-7a6eaca03287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582003} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c33853-ed17-50cd-9365-55cdff773212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582102} {:answer/id #uuid "baf290cb-bee8-57a2-8f68-84f632bad35b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582131} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2e168b-113e-5f39-bd83-a96775488ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582202} {:answer/id #uuid "feea6b1b-cd69-570a-abf4-a0f9fb53e3df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feea6b1b-cd69-570a-abf4-a0f9fb53e3df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582225} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eaf31c9-2b0a-5983-9665-20143d77a00d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8de3c8e0-f48e-5191-a8ca-d1dc2bb45bdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-36972" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{4})+3x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,-2<x\\le 1 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{4})+kx\\, & \\text{ for }\\,1<x<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ exists. What is the value of constant $k?$" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to exist, the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{4})+3x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{4})+3x) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1\\cdot \\pi }{4})+3(1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=4\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{4})+kx.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(4\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{4})+kx) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1\\cdot \\pi }{4})+k(1) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+k \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+k \\\\  & =4+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ 4+k & =5 \\\\ k & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
